WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.810
The main lesson of this course is that people learn through active construction,

00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:05.290
not passive exposure.

00:00:05.290 --> 00:00:09.110
They connect new information to prior knowledge, refine mental models, focus attention,

00:00:09.110 --> 00:00:13.935
manage cognitive load, practice retrieval, receive feedback, and apply skills in context.

00:00:13.935 --> 00:00:15.375
Each piece supports the others.

00:00:15.375 --> 00:00:16.855
Clear explanations matter,

00:00:16.855 --> 00:00:20.570
but explanations alone are rarely enough to create durable performance.

00:00:20.570 --> 00:00:22.760
Strong learning design is practical and structured.

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:24.615
It starts with the capability people need,

00:00:24.615 --> 00:00:28.430
then builds a path toward that capability through examples, sequencing, practice,

00:00:28.430 --> 00:00:30.015
feedback, review, and transfer.

00:00:30.015 --> 00:00:32.560
It respects the limits of attention and working memory.

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:34.320
It uses relevance to support effort.

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:37.875
It creates confidence through achievable challenge rather than empty reassurance.

00:00:37.875 --> 00:00:39.880
It treats mistakes as data for improvement.

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:42.320
The goal is not to make learners consume more content.

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:46.040
The goal is to help them build understanding they can retrieve and skill they can use.

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:48.460
When a learning experience is aligned with real performance,

00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:52.000
learners are more likely to remember what matters, recognize when to use it,

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:53.910
and adapt it when conditions change.

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:57.095
That is the difference between a course that was completed and learning that actually

00:00:57.095 --> 00:00:58.540
travels back to the work.

00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:02.360
As a practical takeaway, look at any learning experience and ask four questions.

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:03.695
What will the learner need to do?

00:01:03.695 --> 00:01:06.665
What prior knowledge or misconception will shape how they understand it?

00:01:06.665 --> 00:01:08.840
Where will they practice and receive useful feedback?

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:12.120
How will the design help them use the skill later, outside the lesson?

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:15.630
Those questions keep the work grounded in capability rather than content volume.
